{"author":{"id":"609bf866d4a5d716c19faf5d617b3bf3de478230d29602d72aad5aa90a80466f"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792948594,"nonce":"EAM1b8zfo0LL6GBV2aTTE9t+84I=","title":"ash --build links only for the host triple and wasm; any other --target fails at the link","message":"Measured with the shared binary (aarch64-apple-darwin host) on test_basic.hl:\n\n--build --target:\n- aarch64-apple-darwin (host): works.\n- wasm32-wasip1: works (own flow: find_wasm_runtime(triple), rust-lld, ash_runtime.o).\n- x86_64-apple-darwin: object emitted, link fails: 'symbol(s) not found for architecture arm64' (the host runtime is linked).\n- x86_64/aarch64-unknown-linux-gnu, x86_64-unknown-linux-musl, x86_64/aarch64-pc-windows-msvc: object emitted, link fails: \"ld: unknown file type in 'out_\u003ctriple\u003e.o'\" (the host linker is handed a foreign object).\n- riscv64gc-unknown-linux-gnu: refused by LLVM ('No available targets are compatible'): Rust's triple name is not LLVM's (riscv64, with +m,+a,+f,+d,+c).\n\n--emit-aot --target works for all of them except the riscv64gc spelling, so lowering and codegen are fine; what is missing is the link step.\n\nThe native link (crates/ash/src/llvm/aot_link.rs) runs the host's cc/clang/gcc against find_runtime(kind), the host's libash_std; nothing in it names a target. A cross build needs (1) the runtime built for the target, found by triple the way find_wasm_runtime does (a \u003ctriple\u003e/ subdirectory beside ash, or --runtime), (2) a linker for the target (clang --target=\u003ctriple\u003e with lld and a sysroot for that target's libc, or zig cc), (3) the triple normalised from Rust's spelling to LLVM's.\n\nOpen question for the owner: what should 'support triples' mean here: cross-linking an executable for another OS/arch from this machine, short names such as linux-x86_64 as in the release assets, several --target in one command, a --list-targets, or all of these.","files":null}]}